  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current code uses a fixed resolution of 500 points and various optimization techniques to minimize the overlap integral. The primary issue is that higher-resolution representations may capture better features to achieve a lower C5 value. The method focuses on leveraging the symmetries and properties of optimal solutions, particularly periodicity. We'll explore different resolutions, improve the projection method, and use a more efficient optimization strategy to escape local optima.\n\nThe core rationale is:\n1. Use periodic solutions by aligning feasible points with irrational densities\n2. Expand initial solutions to higher resolutions via linear interpolation\n3. Optimize using gradient information combined with particle swarm for better exploration\n4. Validate candidates against the original Erd\u0151s constraint\n\nWe prioritize approaches that:\n- Reduce integral overlap beyond the current record\n- Scale efficiently with problem size\n- Maintain mathematical feasibility\n- Exploit known periodic structures\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Resolution n is variable (initial: 100)\n2. Variables: Float array h \u2208 [0,1]^{n}\n3. Constraint: sum(h)*dx = 1 (dx=2/n)\n4. Objective: C5 = max_k \u222b_{0}^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n5. Optimality: Minimize C5 subject to constraint\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Parent validation:\n   - Check initial_h_values if provided (n_orig=500)\n   - Use linear interpolation to expand parents to any resolution\n   - Formulate constraint as \u2211h_i = floor(n/2) for roundness\n\n2. Initialization:\n   a. Use Beatty sequences for unconstrained optimal density\n   b. Golden ratio segments + minimal random jitter\n   c. Latin squares if parent available\n\n3. Core search: \n   - Particle swarm with DIMACS tournament selection\n   - Hamiltonian annealing for large steps\n   - Local projection to maintain feasibility\n   - Adaptive smoothness parameters\n\n4. Candidate acceptance:\n   - Track best candidate globally\n   - Save/eval every 100 iterations\n   - Validate against original objective\n\n5. Search choices:\n   a. Resolutions: 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600\n   b. Objective weight: \u03bc=0.01\n   c. Smoothness \u03b1 = 200000 (diminish by 30% each restart)\n   d. Max iterations: 1000 per phase with adaptive reduction\n\n6. Validation: \n   - Check h_i \u2208 {0,0.5,1} for valid parents\n   - Use floating-point only for better smoothness\n   - Final projection sets h_i \u2208 {0,1} rigorously\n\nCompute budget:\n   - Single resolution pass: 300 sec\n   - Step-up strategy: 1100/|R| sec per round\n   - Timeout when optimizer stabilizes\n   - Initial: 100 runs per resolution\n\nValidation contract:\n   - Return (h_final, c5_bound, n) with:\n     * 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n     * sum(h_i)*dx = 1\n     * C5 \u2264 0.315 (target)\n   - Final check using scipy.correlate",
